right tail, or both tails), just drawn under the t(n - 1) curve rather than the standard normal curve.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"pAozzjkDe9"}],"key":"VhZuSnnqiI"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comments","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"xxa61UjVaR"}],"key":"jEimCYc406"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even though tables exist for the different t distributions, we will only use software to do the calculation for us.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"eQxCoQ4fW5"}],"key":"oUqz09NaTQ"}],"key":"eC9yBMpiXq"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that due to the symmetry of the t distribution, for a given value of the test statistic t, the p-value for the two-sided test is twice as large as the p-value of either of the one-sided tests. The same thing happens when p-values are calculated under the t distribution as when they are calculated under the Z distribution.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"ZngiGW0Pvj"}],"key":"gcMbeC5TVj"}],"key":"r1VBa9XbRq"}],"key":"UidmpxZ3QW"}],"class":"important","key":"aRjPmWieHi"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 4: Drawing Conclusions","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"FR1yQD2goS"}],"identifier":"step-4-drawing-conclusions","label":"Step 4: Drawing Conclusions","html_id":"step-4-drawing-conclusions","implicit":true,"key":"uWtusKzDOo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As usual, based on the p-value (and some significance level of choice) we assess the significance of the results, and draw our conclusions in context.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"ojy0mxpFwz"}],"key":"GuP6qMgbJM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To summarize:","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"nwXR8TU5rh"}],"key":"TBT7OiDf8Y"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The main difference between the z-test and the t-test for the population mean is that we use the sample standard deviation s instead of the unknown population standard deviation ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"LEVOtQoPvW"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\sigma","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">σ</span></span></span></span>","key":"VFXC4ciKQG"},{"type":"text","value":". As a result, the p-values are calculated under the t distribution instead of under the Z distribution. Since we are using software, this doesn’t really impact us practically. However, it is important to understand what is going on behind the scenes, and not just use the software mechanically. This is why we went through the trouble of explaining the t distribution.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"OO1FctnuvC"}],"key":"oUJRxnVTXF"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: The P-value Under the t Distribution","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"jdVQk8ankB"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-the-p-value-under-the-t-distribution","label":"Check Your Understanding: The P-value Under the t Distribution","html_id":"check-your-understanding-the-p-value-under-the-t-distribution","implicit":true,"key":"tZzkQRn9XR"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-25d2578daaee263925d723f08bb887bd.mjs","model":{"question":"A t-test of $H_0$: $\\mu = 20$ vs. $H_a$: $\\mu > 20$ based on $n = 12$ observations yields $t = 2.1$. Under which distribution is the p-value calculated?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The t distribution with 11 degrees of freedom (area to the right of 2.1)","feedback":"Correct! The p-value is the area to the right of 2.1 under the t distribution with 11 degrees of freedom."},{"correct":false,"text":"The standard normal distribution (area to the right of 2.1)","feedback":"Since $\\sigma$ is unknown (a t-test is being used), the standard normal is not the null distribution."},{"correct":false,"text":"The t distribution with 12 degrees of freedom (area to the right of 2.1)","feedback":"The degrees of freedom are $n - 1 = 11$, not 12."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Degrees of freedom = $n - 1$, and the alternative is \"greater than.\"","explanation":""},"key":"GXZhgPfGwI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We are now ready to look at two examples.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"LYx1X4UVcF"}],"key":"ghzfKBPZZ8"}],"key":"spLnHdUqzW"}],"key":"BzbBHAVrrr"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The t-Test Statistic","url":"/pages/p221-m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mea","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"The t-Test for a Mean: Worked Examples","url":"/pages/p223-m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mea","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}